WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.840
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here and today we're
talking about everyone's favorite anime with

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.799
an uncomfortable amount of underage crotch-fondling...

00:00:09.799 --> 00:00:13.860
Akira Toriyama's enduring and much-beloved,
Dragon Ball.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.640
While Dragon Ball may appear to be little
more than a run-of-the-mill adventure show

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:22.900
for kids, the series and its spinoffs actually
have roots in some pretty hefty philosophical

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:23.900
material.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.800
Many fans are aware that, in creating the
earliest episodes of Dragon Ball, Toriyama

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.360
drew inspiration from a five-hundred-year-old
Chinese classic, Journey to the West.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:36.710
The superficial similarities are easy to spot
– both feature a group of four adventurers

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:41.480
in search of powerful magical objects, among
them a monkey-like creature with a magic staff,

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.690
a horny piggish-looking fellow, et cetera.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:48.740
Often overlooked, however, is the fact that
Dragon Ball, much like Journey to the West,

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.200
can be read as an explicitly Buddhist allegory.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.280
Indeed, – a show that many of us watched
in our underwear on Saturday mornings while

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.690
wolfing down Cookie Crisp – offers a surprising
take on fundamental elements of Buddhist doctrine.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:04.280
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the Philosophy
of Dragon Ball.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.470
(Note for the die-hard fans keeping score
at home: since a full discussion of the extended

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:12.280
Dragon Ball universe would take quite a while,
today we’ll focus mainly on those episodes

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:17.332
of the original anime most heavily influenced
by Journey to the West - the thirteen episodes

00:01:17.332 --> 00:01:19.220
comprising the Emperor Pilaf saga.)

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:21.570
But first, a quick refresher.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:26.391
Dragon Ball follows Goku, a young boy with
a monkey tail and superhuman strength, and

00:01:26.391 --> 00:01:30.640
Bulma, a girl who plans to collect the Dragon
Balls in order to wish for a new boyfriend.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:35.810
Along the way, they team up with Oolong, a
shape-shifting pig, and Yamcha, a desert-dwelling

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:37.920
bandit with a crippling fear of women.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.630
They are in a race against time to collect
all seven Dragon Balls before the evil Emperor

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.190
Pilaf can snag them for himself.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:48.619
At the last moment, when Pilaf has gathered
all of the Dragon Balls and is about to wish

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:55.000
for world domination, Oolong saves the day
by sabotaging his wish: "I want to have the

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.850
world-" "-the world's most comfortable pair
of ultra soft UNDERWEAR!!!"

00:01:57.850 --> 00:01:58.869
Remember that face, boys and girls.

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:00.900
This moment will be an important part of our
discussion later on.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:01.900
REMEMBER IT.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.920
Journey to the West follows Tripitika, a Buddhist
monk, and his three disciples, Monkey, Pigsy,

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.920
and Sandy, on their quest to locate and bring
back sacred Buddhist scrolls to the Chinese

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:11.920
court.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.829
When the pilgrims arrive at the Buddha's paradise,
the scrolls they receive turn out to be completely

00:02:15.829 --> 00:02:16.840
blank.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:20.829
While Journey to the West involves characters
searching for a magical object, there’s

00:02:20.829 --> 00:02:23.549
more to it than your basic (annoying) fetch
quest.

00:02:23.549 --> 00:02:28.370
The novel is often read as a Buddhist allegory,
one concerned with spiritual enlightenment.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:32.609
The characters move from a place of ignorance
to one of insight as they get closer to their

00:02:32.609 --> 00:02:33.609
destination.

00:02:33.609 --> 00:02:36.499
Surprisingly enough, the same applies to Dragon
Ball.

00:02:36.499 --> 00:02:40.629
Both Dragon Ball and Journey to the West have
their philosophical grounding in 'Mahayana'

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:41.629
Buddhism.

00:02:41.629 --> 00:02:45.900
To vastly oversimplify, the Mahayana approach
to Buddhism is distinguished from the earlier

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.499
'Theravada' school by its emphasis on the
collective enlightenment of all sentient beings

00:02:50.499 --> 00:02:53.970
in the universe, rather than that of any one
individual.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:58.129
Accordingly, Dragon Ball puts a premium on
acts of 'compassion,' and on the growth of

00:02:58.129 --> 00:02:59.569
the group as a whole.

00:02:59.569 --> 00:03:03.420
When the heroes of Dragon Ball put others
first, they advance in their quest.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:07.291
When they act selfishly and refuse to see
things from others' points of view, their

00:03:07.291 --> 00:03:09.010
progress is delayed.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:18.329
In the second episode, Goku and Bulma encounter
a beached Turtle who explains that he 

00:03:18.329 --> 00:03:19.959
is lost.

00:03:19.959 --> 00:03:24.190
While Goku wants to put their journey on hold
in order to help the turtle, Bulma wants to

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:29.299
get right back to her quest to find the Dragon
Balls.

00:03:29.299 --> 00:03:36.239
However, Goku's act of compassion unexpectedly
turns out to be the most direct route to the

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:37.469
next Dragon Ball.

00:03:37.469 --> 00:03:41.019
Enter Master Roshi, hermit and pervert extraordinaire.

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:45.620
Roshi is grateful to Goku for returning the
turtle to the sea, and gives Goku his iconic

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.510
Flying Nimbus cloud as a reward.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:51.549
He also happens to own a Dragon Ball, which
he is willing to give to Bulma in exchange

00:03:51.549 --> 00:03:53.370
for...well.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:59.370
It's a weird show, guys.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:04.229
The ethical implications of trading peep shows
for magical jewelry aside, the fact remains

00:04:04.229 --> 00:04:09.720
that Bulma and Goku find the Dragon Ball because
Goku is willing to delay his own gratification

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.049
in order to help out a fellow being in need.

00:04:13.049 --> 00:04:22.420
Now that I think about it, as the series goes
on, Goku spends a lot of time dragging around

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:27.340
his useless childhood friends while doing
all the work – lookin’ at you, Krillin.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:31.450
If that ain’t compassion, I’m not sure
what is.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:35.940
This correlation between ethical behavior
and speed of progress is lifted straight from

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:40.560
Journey to the West, in which a lack of compassion
on the part of the pilgrims causes delays

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.080
that can only be resolved through compassion
and understanding.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:48.430
Monkey often gets in fights with monsters,
only to realize that those monsters were supposed

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:49.530
to help him.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:54.070
Both Pigsy and Sandy start out as foes, only
to eventually become allies.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:58.040
This goes part of the way toward explaining
early Dragon Ball’s unexpected attitude

00:04:58.040 --> 00:04:59.320
toward violence.

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:05.410
For a franchise that would eventually go on
to feature scenes such as this.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:06.990
This.

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.570
This.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:10.160
This.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.930
And this.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:17.950
Dragon Ball, in its earliest episodes, has
a surprising lack of violence.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:23.010
Like Journey to the West, the show is preoccupied
with a particular Buddhist conception of nonviolence.

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:27.630
Violence is not universally wrong so much
as it is cumbersome – to some degree, it

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.390
does get in the way of insight.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:32.770
Conversely, compassion facilitates spiritual
progress.

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:37.580
This understanding of 'violence as roadblock'
is most clearly displayed with the Ox King.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:41.500
When the group first meets the Ox King, Goku
tries to beat him up in order to clear the

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:44.490
pathway up Fire Mountain and take his Dragon
Ball.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:47.270
The enormous man barely feels Goku's attacks.

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:52.800
For the duration of this fight, everyone involved
is stuck in an unproductive, pointless cycle

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.170
that gets the pilgrims nowhere.

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:59.320
It is only when Goku and the Ox King begin
to understand each other's needs through conversation

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:11.170
that the team gets any closer to their goal.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:14.930
Compassion and understanding, rather than
violence, lead to the trade that eventually

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:17.140
nets the team another Dragon Ball.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:21.120
Which is all well and good when you're dealing
with a scary but ultimately harmless threat

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:22.450
like the Ox King.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:26.070
But how should our heroes approach a threat
that can't be reasoned with?

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:29.900
According to writer Paul R. Fleischman, the
Buddhist approach to nonviolence makes room

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:35.430
for this kind of scenario: "permitting someone
else to perpetrate harm without consequences

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:37.210
is not nonviolence".

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:42.690
Defending innocent lives with violent means
is hardly ideal, but it is preferable to rigid

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:43.690
pacifism.

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:48.080
In the last few episodes of the Emperor Pilaf
saga, the team is confronted with an existential

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:52.530
threats when Goku turns into a giant ape at
the sight of the full moon and threatens to

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:53.530
eat Bulma.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:59.110
So Yamcha, Puar, and Oolong cut off Goku's
tail to save Bulma and stop Goku from doing

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:00.790
something he would later regret.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:04.340
Their limited use of violence prevents worse
violence down the road.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:06.770
This way, at least, nobody dies.

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:11.440
This more nuanced understanding of violence
would pop up in Toriyama's later work as well.

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:31.560
Listen to Goku's description of himself in
response to Frieza's question, "What are you?"

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:35.780
Unlike the various villains that maim and
murder throughout the series, Goku commits

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:38.050
violent acts to preserve peace.

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:42.730
Perhaps most interesting is how Dragon Ball
handles the Buddhist concept of 'emptiness,'

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:47.210
the idea that things in themselves have no
inherent or fixed essence.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:51.710
Early on, Toriyama asks us to consider this
concept in relation to the Dragon Balls: "These

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:54.660
gems can be used for great things.

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:55.660
And not so great."

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:59.780
From the start, the series points out that
the Dragon Balls are neither inherently good,

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:04.820
bad, or anything in between – they are simply
a store of potential energy used to fulfil

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:05.820
wishes.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:09.520
Even so, the heroes revere them as a life-changing
miracle.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:13.120
Their disposition towards the Dragon Balls
mirrors the pilgrims' attitude toward the

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.520
sacred scrolls in Journey to the West.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:19.380
When the Buddha's assistants actually give
them scrolls that are BLANK, they are more

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:21.230
than a little pissed off.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:22.400
But they shouldn't be.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:27.050
Scholar Andrew Hui argues that "[t]he blankness
of the scrolls is a material manifestation

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:31.820
of the Buddhist doctrine of emptiness, a sign
pointing to the […] nature of ultimate reality"

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:32.820
(Hui 3).

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:36.389
The Buddha himself explains to the pilgrims
that "As a matter of fact, it is such blank

00:08:36.389 --> 00:08:39.419
scrolls as these that are the true scriptures"
(Cheng-en, Wu).

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:43.610
By undercutting their expectations, the Buddha
provides the pilgrims with a profound lesson

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:48.110
about the nature of reality, namely that it's
empty of any inherent essence.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:51.649
In this way, the prank actually advances their
spiritual progress.

00:08:51.649 --> 00:08:54.300
A similar bait-and-switch occurs in Dragon
Ball.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:55.850
Remember Oolong's panty wish?

00:08:55.850 --> 00:09:00.670
By ruining Pilaf's wish, Oolong undercuts
the heroes' expectations, turning something

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:04.810
as momentous as the summoning of the Eternal
Dragon into a joke.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:09.059
While the Dragon Balls have provided Oolong
with a pair of panties, they haven't changed

00:09:09.059 --> 00:09:11.579
anyone's life in any meaningful way.

00:09:11.579 --> 00:09:13.670
Which is not to say the journey was for nothing.

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:14.670
Far from it.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:19.379
In both the show and the novel, the revelation
of the quest's inherent emptiness serves as

00:09:19.379 --> 00:09:22.779
the catalyst for growth beyond what the characters
expected.

00:09:22.779 --> 00:09:26.899
In the final chapters of Journey to the West,
the pilgrims are made into high ranking members

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:30.980
of the Buddha's paradise, while the heroes
of Dragon Ball gain insight into the true

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:35.500
scope of their various problems, which turn
out to be not as terrible or permanent as

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:36.500
they'd thought.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:40.430
Bulma and Yamcha had planned to use the Dragon
Balls to find a boyfriend and cure social

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:41.649
anxiety, respectively.

00:09:41.649 --> 00:09:46.019
But After Oolong pulls his stunt, they realize
the solution to their mutual problem has been

00:09:46.019 --> 00:09:47.519
in front of them the whole time.

00:09:47.519 --> 00:09:49.709
They agree to get together on the spot.

00:09:49.709 --> 00:09:53.550
Bulma lets go of her need for perfection,
and Yamcha lets go of his fear.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:58.100
Even Oolong, who we first meet kidnapping
young girls to staff his would-be harem, has

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:01.809
learned to find happiness in thinking about
others besides himself.

00:10:01.809 --> 00:10:06.399
When asked whether he will accompany Yamcha
and Bulma to the city, he says: "Oh well,

00:10:06.399 --> 00:10:08.059
I guess I'll go.

00:10:08.059 --> 00:10:09.720
You guys need me."

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:12.319
So – turtles, flashing, panties, and more.

00:10:12.319 --> 00:10:15.369
All in all, a pretty weird way of getting
a religious message across.

00:10:15.369 --> 00:10:17.220
But, that's kind of the point.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:21.720
Mahayana Buddhism, with its emphasis on teaching
and collective enlightenment, is big into

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:26.570
the concept of 'skillful means,' or the guiding
of sentient beings toward enlightenment according

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:30.899
to their current abilities – in other words,
meeting people where they're at.

00:10:30.899 --> 00:10:35.050
One way to meet people where they're at is
to dispense with dry lecturing and tell them

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:40.170
a damn good story, and that's exactly what
Dragon Ball and Journey to the West do.

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:45.129
With engaging plotlines, creative characters,
and writing that is alternately tense and

00:10:45.129 --> 00:10:50.230
hilarious, Dragon Ball successfully weaves
Buddhist morals into a story that people love

00:10:50.230 --> 00:11:20.209
to come back to again 

00:11:20.209 --> 00:11:57.699
and again.

